  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Divers found seventy ancient ships stacked in one bay. Abu Qir, east of Alexandria, seven kilometres offshore and six metres down.",
        "visual_query": "underwater ruins mediterranean diver",
        "visual_fallback": "scuba diver"
      },
      {
        "narration": "This was Thonis-Heracleion, Egypt's gateway for foreign trade for centuries before Alexandria existed. Temples, gold coins, a statue holding four loaves of bread.",
        "visual_query": "ancient egyptian temple columns",
        "visual_fallback": "stone columns"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A granite slab carried a decree of Nectanebo the First and the city's real Egyptian name. So how does an entire port disappear?",
        "visual_query": "egyptian hieroglyph stone carving",
        "visual_fallback": "carved stone"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The ground gave way. Around 101 BC heavy flooding liquefied the clay under the central island, and earthquakes and rising sea finished the job.",
        "visual_query": "nile delta shallow water",
        "visual_fallback": "muddy shore"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Franck Goddio's team located the city in 2000, after five years of searching the bay. He reckons about five percent has been excavated.",
        "visual_query": "diver surveying seabed sand",
        "visual_fallback": "seabed sand"
      }
    ],
